From: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_qca: let the rails fall before retrying a power
 on

When the controller crashes -- which this driver does on purpose, sending a
crash buffer to collect a memory dump -- qca_setup() retries the power-on up
to MAX_INIT_RETRIES times. Each retry calls qca_power_off(), closes and
reopens the serdev, and powers the chip back on.

On WCN399x boards that have no bt_en GPIO, the whole of qca_power_off() is a
power pulse plus qca_regulator_disable(). The regulators going away are the
*only* thing that resets the controller. And the retry re-enabled them
immediately: microseconds later, with the rails still charged. From the
controller's point of view that is not a power cycle at all.

Measured on a Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC (sm7150, WCN3990), three separate incidents:
after the forced crash the chip never came back, with the same sequence every
time --

  Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn399x
  Bluetooth: hci0: Frame reassembly failed (-84)
  Bluetooth: hci0: Reading QCA version information failed (-110)
  Bluetooth: hci0: Retry BT power ON:0
  ... :1, :2, and then
  Bluetooth: hci0: Opcode 0x0c03 failed: -110

The adapter stayed dead until the machine was rebooted. Worse, on this phone
touching Bluetooth afterwards -- a reconnect attempt from the desktop, or an
rfkill block -- hung or reset the whole machine, so a dead controller was not
merely a missing adapter.

Give the rails 250 ms to discharge before retrying. It only affects the
failure path, which already spends about ten seconds per attempt timing out,
so the added delay is not measurable next to it.

Note this is not a fix for the crash itself, only for the recovery from it:
the controller should come back after being deliberately crashed, and now it
gets a real power cycle in which to do so.
---
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -52,6 +52,13 @@
 #define IBS_HOST_TX_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS	2000
 #define CMD_TRANS_TIMEOUT_MS		100
 #define MEMDUMP_TIMEOUT_MS		8000
+/* Time to let the SoC rails actually discharge before powering it back on.
+ * On boards where the WCN399x has no bt_en GPIO -- its only reset is the
+ * regulators going away -- the retry path toggled them back on within
+ * microseconds, which is not a power cycle from the controller's point of
+ * view.
+ */
+#define POWER_OFF_SETTLE_MS		250
 #define IBS_DISABLE_SSR_TIMEOUT_MS \
 	(MEMDUMP_TIMEOUT_MS + FW_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS)
 #define FW_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS		3000
@@ -2106,6 +2113,21 @@
 
 		if (retries < MAX_INIT_RETRIES) {
 			bt_dev_warn(hdev, "Retry BT power ON:%d", retries);
+
+			/* qca_power_off() just dropped the regulators and
+			 * returned; on WCN399x boards without a bt_en GPIO
+			 * that is the *only* way the controller is reset, and
+			 * we were re-enabling them immediately. Give the rails
+			 * time to fall so the retry starts from a controller
+			 * that really did power down. Without this, every
+			 * retry after a controller crash came back with
+			 * "Frame reassembly failed (-84)" and then -110 on
+			 * every read of the version information -- the chip
+			 * never returned, and the adapter stayed dead until
+			 * the machine was rebooted.
+			 */
+			msleep(POWER_OFF_SETTLE_MS);
+
 			if (hu->serdev) {
 				serdev_device_close(hu->serdev);
 				ret = serdev_device_open(hu->serdev);
